# Commit Rules

## Core Principles

- Every commit must represent one clear change point. Do not combine unrelated requirements, bug fixes, or refactors in the same commit.
- Every commit must link to one primary document entry: `docs/changes/active/<change-key>/`, `docs/issues/`, `docs/workflow/01-requirements/README.md`, or another explicit requirement / design / verification document.
- Commit language should follow the project documentation language. Chinese projects use Chinese titles and bodies; English projects use English titles and bodies.
- The commit body must record purpose, modified scope, impact scope, and test status. A short title alone is not enough.
- Linked items or status items with no content must omit the whole line. Do not write `None`, `Not applicable`, `N/A`, or empty placeholders.
- Before committing, finish the required analyze / converge checks and confirm docs, tests, and change records are synced.

## Title Format

```text
<type>: <short title>
```

Common `type` values:

- `feat`: new feature, new requirement, or capability expansion
- `fix`: bug fix or failure-path fix
- `docs`: documentation, rule, example, or explanation change
- `test`: tests, regression suites, fixtures, or coverage mapping
- `refactor`: structure change without external behavior changes
- `chore`: build, scripts, dependencies, or repository maintenance
- `release`: release notes, version records, or delivery summary

Title requirements:

- Use the current project language and write a readable sentence.
- Describe the result, not only the action.
- Keep the title under 72 characters; put details in the body.

## Body Format

```text
Summary:
One sentence explaining the core purpose of this commit.

Links:
- Linked Issue: #123 or docs/issues/README.md#...
- Change: docs/changes/active/CHG-0001-xxx/
- Requirement: FR-001 / AC-001 / docs/workflow/01-requirements/README.md
- Design: DES-001 / docs/workflow/02-design/README.md
- Verification: TEST-001 / docs/workflow/04-verification/README.md

Modified scope:
- [file or module 1]
- [file or module 2]

Impact scope:
- [affected feature, module, user flow, or release surface]

Test status:
- [test commands run and results]
- [tests not run and why]
- [residual risks or manual review points]

Detailed changes:
- [specific change 1]
- [specific change 2]
```

If an item has no content, omit the whole line. Do not write `None`, `Not applicable`, `N/A`, or similar empty values.

Example:

```text
Links:
- Change: docs/changes/completed/CHG-20260624-001-homepage-hero/
- Frontend: frontend/web@bd4be12
```

Do not write:

```text
Links:
- Change: docs/changes/completed/CHG-20260624-001-homepage-hero/
- Requirement: None
- Design: None
- Test: None
```

## Linking Rules

- One commit can bind to only one primary change workspace or one primary Issue.
- One commit may reference multiple `FR-*` / `DES-*` / `TEST-*` IDs, but it must have one main objective.
- New requirements, bug fixes, refactors, and workflow changes must link to `docs/changes/active/<change-key>/` or the corresponding completed / legacy record.
- Bug fixes must link to an Issue or bug record, and test status must mention regression verification.
- Documentation-only commits must still state the linked rule, requirement, design, or change document.
- If no Issue or change workspace exists, create the document record before committing.

## Scope Rules

- One commit solves one requirement, one bug, one refactor target, or one documentation rule change.
- Do not combine “implement feature + fix unrelated bug + format the whole repository” in one commit.
- If one work item must touch multiple modules, explain in the body why those modules belong to the same change point.
- Only record files, modules, and impact surfaces actually touched by the commit. Do not write broad project vision statements.

## Test Status Rules

- `Test status` must include the real commands run, such as `bash tests/spec-init.sh`.
- Do not commit a failed test state as complete unless the commit intentionally records a failing test or baseline and the body says so.
- If tests were not run, explain why, such as “README-only change; links and example formatting checked manually.”
- High-risk paths, bug fixes, data model changes, API contracts, and permission boundaries must mention regression coverage and residual risk.

## Example

```text
docs: add commit rules and document linking requirements

Summary:
Add structured commit rules to the project scaffold so each commit records linked docs, modified scope, impact scope, and test status.

Links:
- Change: docs/changes/active/CHG-0001-template/
- Requirement: FR-001 / docs/workflow/01-requirements/README.md
- Design: DES-001 / docs/workflow/02-design/README.md
- Verification: TEST-001 / docs/workflow/04-verification/README.md

Modified scope:
- skills/spec-init/assets/templates/project/zh/docs/rules/commit-rules.md.tmpl
- skills/spec-init/assets/templates/project/en/docs/rules/commit-rules.md.tmpl
- skills/spec-init/scripts/spec-init.sh
- tests/spec-init.sh

Impact scope:
- Newly initialized projects generate commit rule docs
- Agents and developers must include a structured body when committing changes

Test status:
- Run: bash tests/spec-init.sh, passed
- Residual risk: existing projects need to copy this rule manually

Detailed changes:
- Add the `docs/rules/commit-rules.md` template
- Register commit rules in rule guides, AGENTS, README, and smoke tests
- Require one primary change point per commit and explicit test status
```
